WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.574
Another ticket arrives.

2
00:00:01.574 --> 00:00:05.512
A customer thinks they were charged twice for
their May invoice.

3
00:00:05.512 --> 00:00:11.240
Their bank shows two debits of two thousand four
hundred and ninety nine rupees, and they

4
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.030
want help with a refund.

5
00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:18.400
A classification helps, but the useful outcome
is getting this request to the right team.

6
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.582
Most cases are familiar.

7
00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:22.031
Some need investigation.

8
00:00:22.031 --> 00:00:29.275
You need a continuing workflow that can judge,
investigate, execute, and record what actually
happened.

9
00:00:29.525 --> 00:00:33.450
How much of that machinery should every
application rebuild?

10
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:42.647
This is for developers turning judgment into
work: routing tickets, filtering information,
validating requests, and other

11
00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:44.692
applications with useful choices.

12
00:00:44.942 --> 00:00:47.732
Your advantage is knowing your domain.

13
00:00:47.732 --> 00:00:49.127
Which facts matter?

14
00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:50.522
Which action follows?

15
00:00:50.522 --> 00:00:51.917
What deserves investigation?

16
00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:59.185
The goal is to jump-start the agent around that
logic: reuse models, tools, records, and deeper

17
00:00:59.185 --> 00:01:01.817
help, while keeping application decisions yours.

18
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:06.133
Daniel Kahneman described two modes of thinking.

19
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:10.848
System One is automatic: familiar patterns come
to mind easily.

20
00:01:10.848 --> 00:01:16.033
System Two takes deliberate effort, like working
through an unfamiliar problem.

21
00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:18.474
Imagine a new support colleague.

22
00:01:18.474 --> 00:01:24.171
They read the policy, think through a refund
request, and propose a team.

23
00:01:24.171 --> 00:01:27.676
A supervisor checks the decision and explains
mistakes.

24
00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:32.058
After repeated practice, familiar charge issues
become easy to recognize.

25
00:01:32.308 --> 00:01:37.458
That learning needs patterns that can actually
be learned, and reliable feedback.

26
00:01:37.458 --> 00:01:40.462
An unusual case still deserves deliberate
thought.

27
00:01:40.462 --> 00:01:47.329
The lesson is to develop useful recognition and
know its limits, not to trust every first

28
00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:47.758
impression.

29
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:53.970
A System One model such as Jev supplies a
related building block.

30
00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.306
Information and typed questions go in; usable
judgments come out.

31
00:01:58.306 --> 00:02:01.342
A Choice selects payments, technical, or SOS.

32
00:02:01.592 --> 00:02:06.195
A Noul answers a yes-or-no question with a
probability.

33
00:02:06.195 --> 00:02:09.264
Choice also returns probabilities and
confidence.

34
00:02:09.264 --> 00:02:13.867
Concentrated answers look decisive; confidence
does not prove correctness.

35
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:17.870
A System Two language model can plan and
investigate.

36
00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:23.709
The aim is lower routine cost and latency, with
deeper reasoning available when needed.

37
00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:25.377
Jev is already trained.

38
00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:29.547
We configure and evaluate inputs and criteria,
not its weights.

39
00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:32.467
This analogy describes a division of work.

40
00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:35.296
Suppose the classifier works.

41
00:02:35.296 --> 00:02:41.415
You still need to execute assignment, check its
real result, feed evidence into the next
decision,

42
00:02:41.415 --> 00:02:44.092
and know when the task is finished.

43
00:02:44.342 --> 00:02:50.039
Add investigation: a helper needs a goal, scoped
tools, and a findings return.

44
00:02:50.039 --> 00:02:51.792
The session needs records.

45
00:02:52.042 --> 00:02:55.387
You can build a custom agent for every product.

46
00:02:55.387 --> 00:03:00.592
Or reuse that machinery and concentrate on the
rules that make your application useful.

47
00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:12.033
That foundation is Pi: a mature agent harness.

48
00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:18.567
In its ordinary loop, an LLM receives a goal and
tools, calls a tool, reads its

49
00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:21.017
result, and continues until it responds.

50
00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:27.308
Pi already exposes classifiers such as Jev to
extensions and model-callable tools.

51
00:03:27.308 --> 00:03:32.342
That makes classification available, without
making it the ongoing driver.

52
00:03:32.592 --> 00:03:38.767
Turbo is a Pi extension that makes typed
judgment drive the next step.

53
00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:44.467
Native classifier access sits behind a custom
provider and selectable virtual model.

54
00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:46.367
Pi still executes tools.

55
00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:51.592
Other Pi extensions and connected MCP tools
remain usable alongside Turbo.

56
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.395
Now the complete flow.

57
00:03:54.395 --> 00:04:00.677
Our invoice ticket comes with routing policy and
verified payment facts: two charges settled.

58
00:04:00.677 --> 00:04:02.472
System One selects payments.

59
00:04:02.472 --> 00:04:05.164
Pi assigns T42 to that team.

60
00:04:05.164 --> 00:04:10.100
Its receipt confirms the assignment, and the
workflow finishes this request.

61
00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:17.234
Then a follow-up arrives: checkout says payment
failed, and the customer’s team cannot access
the service.

62
00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:19.386
Is this the same issue?

63
00:04:19.386 --> 00:04:21.967
When enabled, System One requests investigation.

64
00:04:21.967 --> 00:04:26.700
The helper checks the order and service status
through permitted tools.

65
00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:31.089
Outage evidence returns to preparation and the
next judgment.

66
00:04:31.089 --> 00:04:34.308
System One selects SOS under your policy.

67
00:04:34.308 --> 00:04:37.527
Pi assigns and records the new result.

68
00:04:37.527 --> 00:04:39.366
System Two supplied help.

69
00:04:39.366 --> 00:04:42.125
System One resumed the decision flow.

70
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:44.015
Zoom in.

71
00:04:44.015 --> 00:04:50.183
Your functions define relevant facts, questions,
decision criteria, and what an answer should do.

72
00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:56.031
Turbo carries that decision through
classification, action, consultation, or
completion.

73
00:04:56.031 --> 00:05:00.508
It checks mechanical constraints and keeps
control records.

74
00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:05.506
Pi owns the session, tools, execution hooks, and
continuation.

75
00:05:05.506 --> 00:05:08.671
Ticket categories live in your application.

76
00:05:08.671 --> 00:05:11.308
That separation makes Turbo reusable.

77
00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:15.627
Configure your integration and System One
classifier.

78
00:05:15.627 --> 00:05:20.333
Optionally enable a System Two model and its
allowed tools.

79
00:05:20.583 --> 00:05:26.229
Register business operations through Pi, an MCP
connection, or another extension.

80
00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:29.308
Your assignment tool can already exist.

81
00:05:29.558 --> 00:05:32.408
Map the capabilities your workflow needs.

82
00:05:32.408 --> 00:05:37.158
The helper gets its granted subset; Pi hooks
still apply.

83
00:05:37.158 --> 00:05:39.058
Existing integrations remain available.

84
00:05:40.067 --> 00:05:41.381
Now your code.

85
00:05:41.381 --> 00:05:46.636
Prepare the next judgment: load the ticket,
routing policy, and relevant evidence.

86
00:05:46.636 --> 00:05:51.892
If a real assignment receipt confirms the
outcome, return a final decision.

87
00:05:52.142 --> 00:05:54.027
Otherwise, define the question.

88
00:05:54.027 --> 00:06:01.568
Ask for the next step and describe each choice:
payments, technical, SOS, or investigation when
help

89
00:06:01.568 --> 00:06:02.510
is available.

90
00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:04.867
Your criteria explain the boundaries.

91
00:06:05.117 --> 00:06:08.445
Map the returned answer to an exact action.

92
00:06:08.445 --> 00:06:13.437
Payments becomes the registered assignment tool
with this ticket ID and team.

93
00:06:13.437 --> 00:06:15.517
The tool performs the work.

94
00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:18.604
Register the preparation function through Turbo.

95
00:06:18.604 --> 00:06:22.859
Its flow calls your functions again after
results arrive.

96
00:06:22.859 --> 00:06:26.642
You supply meaning and bindings; Pi supplies
execution.

97
00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:36.547
For investigation, return a consultation: goal,
supplied facts, completion condition, and exact
tool grants.

98
00:06:36.547 --> 00:06:40.712
The built-in helper runs a scoped native Pi
agent.

99
00:06:40.712 --> 00:06:46.267
Its LLM can take several tool steps until it
finishes the subtask.

100
00:06:46.517 --> 00:06:49.900
Findings and real tool outcomes return to
preparation.

101
00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.860
You choose evidence for the next judgment.

102
00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.667
The classifier cannot remember facts it was
never given.

103
00:06:56.917 --> 00:06:58.433
Pi keeps history.

104
00:06:58.433 --> 00:06:59.950
Turbo tracks execution.

105
00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:05.006
Your code manages business state and context in
version one.

106
00:07:05.006 --> 00:07:10.567
Persistent records are the foundation; automatic
crash continuation remains separate work.

107
00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:15.619
You bring rules, relevant state, and tool
bindings.

108
00:07:15.619 --> 00:07:19.670
Turbo supplies judgment-driven flow and optional
deeper help.

109
00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:25.242
Pi supplies execution and records, alongside
your extensions and MCP tools.

110
00:07:25.492 --> 00:07:29.000
An independent news agent can reuse these seams.

111
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:36.017
Start with one bounded workflow, evaluate real
cases, and focus on what makes your application
useful.
